Now I have the full picture. This is MSTest 4.1.0 on .NET 10, with the class explicitly marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`. Let me deliver the audit.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration-test calibration applied** (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Smell:** Conditional Test Logic (testsmells.org #1)
**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);   // path A
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);       // path B
}
```

**Problem:** Only one branch ever executes per run. If the production type changes, the other path silently stops being tested. This hides gaps even in integration tests — integration calibration does not excuse conditional logic.

**Fix — split into two dedicated tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremiumUser_HasDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — one type path is never validated, and you won't know which one without reading the test.

---

#### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Smell:** Unknown Test / Assertion-Free Test (testsmells.org #4)
**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 112)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion — passes as long as no exception is thrown
}
```

**Problem:** This test proves the code doesn't throw, but says nothing about whether 100 rows actually persisted. A `ListAll()` returning 0 rows would still pass.

**Fix — add a real assertion:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False pass — production code could silently swallow inserts and this test would still be green.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Sleepy Test

**Smell:** Sleepy Test (testsmells.org #3)
**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 81)
**Severity note:** Downgraded from High → Medium per integration-test calibration. Still a real smell because the 3-second sleep makes the suite slower and the timing is non-deterministic.

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);   // blocks for 3 full seconds

Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead of a fixed sleep:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> WaitUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 3 s wasted per run even when the event fires in 50 ms; on slow CI boxes the 3 s may not be enough → flaky failure.

---

#### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Smell:** Assertion Roulette (testsmells.org extended catalog)
**Location:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
// 4 assertions, 0 failure messages
```

**Problem:** When any of these fails in CI, the MSTest output says `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<(null)>.` with no context about *which property* was wrong. With 4 bare assertions the developer must read the test source to diagnose.

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be assigned on insert");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage — a CI failure requires opening the source file instead of reading the log.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-structured:

| Test | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single behavior, clean null check |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count of 3 is self-documenting (3 items just inserted) — no Magic Number smell |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Properly awaited, clear assertion, good concurrency coverage |

The `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` setup is tight — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test, so there is **no General Fixture smell**. Using in-memory SQLite means **no Mystery Guest** (hermetic, no external resources). No tests are skipped, no try/catch blocks, no ToString comparisons, no duplicate assertions, no redundant prints, and the class name is meaningful.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, …)` to `BulkInsert` | 2 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** | Split `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` into 2 tests | 10 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** | Replace `Thread.Sleep(3000)` with poll-and-timeout helper | 15 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** | Add message strings to the 4 assertions in `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | 2 min |

Fixes 1 and 4 are quick wins you can ship today. Fix 2 requires understanding the `PremiumUser` type hierarchy. Fix 3 needs a small helper method but eliminates 3 seconds from every test run.
